#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049144 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048293 :topic/knowledge-points [{:knowledge-point/canonical-example {:question/problem "For the parametric curve\n\n$$\nx=3t, \\,\\,\\, y=t^3, \\quad -3\\leq t \\leq 3,\n$$\n\nfill in the following table of values.\n\n| **$t$** | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "First, we work out the $x$-values using the formula $x=3t.$ This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-9$ | $-6$ | $-3$ | $0$ | $3$ | $6$ | $9$ |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow, we work out the $y$-values using $y=t^3.$ This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-9$ | $-6$ | $-3$ | $0$ | $3$ | $6$ | $9$ |\n| $y$ | $-27$ | $-8$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $8$ | $27$ |"} :knowledge-point/title "Filling a Table of Values for a Parametric Curve"} {:knowledge-point/canonical-example {:question/problem "Plot the graph of the parametrically defined curve\n\n$$\nx=t(t-6), \\,\\,\\, y=t^3, \\quad -2\\leq t \\leq 2.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We need to create a table of values and then sketch the curve. First, we work out the $x$-values using the formula $x=t(t-6).$ This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $16$ | $7$ | $0$ | $-5$ | $-8$ |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow, we work out the $y$ values using $y=t^3.$ This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $16$ | $7$ | $0$ | $-5$ | $-8$ |\n| $y$ | $-8$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $8$ |\n\nPlotting these points, we obtain the following curve:\n\n![](images/f1/f15f25af0c4804dc811a0ab231ba2ef3ecce2ecd12501a1f2d8b9e34b71a505b.png)"} :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Curve Defined Parametrically"} {:knowledge-point/canonical-example {:question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=t^2-6t, \\,\\,\\, y=t^4+4, \\quad t \\in (-\\infty , \\infty),\n$$\n\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ we can start by completing the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={t}^{2}-6t \\\\  & ={(t-3)}^{2}-(3)^{2} \\\\  & ={(t-3)}^{2}-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\left( t-3\\right)^2 \\geq 0,$ we can conclude that $x \\geq -9.$\n\n- To find the range of $y=t^4+4,$ notice that $t^4 \\geq 0,$ and consequently $y \\geq 4.$\n\nTherefore, the ranges are $x \\geq -9$ and $y \\geq 4.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Range of a Parametric Equation"} {:knowledge-point/canonical-example {:question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx = 4\\cos\\theta, \\,\\,\\, y = 2\\sin\\theta, \\quad 0\\leq \\theta <     2\\pi,\n$$\n\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ notice that for $0\\leq\\theta <  2\\pi,$ we have $-1 \\leq \\cos{\\theta} \\leq 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -4 & \\le 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 4 \\\\ -4 & \\le x\\le 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-4 \\leq x \\leq 4.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ notice that for $0\\leq\\theta <  2\\pi,$ we have $-1 \\leq \\sin{\\theta} \\leq 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -2 & \\le 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 2 \\\\ -2 & \\le y\\le 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-2 \\leq y \\leq 2.$\n\nTherefore, the ranges are $-4 \\leq x \\leq 4$ and $-2 \\leq y \\leq 2.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Range of a Parametric Equation Defined in Terms of Trigonometric Functions"}] :topic/math-academy-id 803 :topic/title "Graphing Curves Defined Parametrically"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048362 :topic/knowledge-points [{:knowledge-point/canonical-example {:question/problem "Write $2\\sin{15^\\circ} \\cos{15^\\circ}$ as a single trigonometric ratio." :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\sin{x}\\cos{x} = \\sin 2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=15^\\circ$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡{15}^{∘}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{15}^{∘} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot {15}^{∘})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Using the Double-Angle Formula to Simplify Products"} {:knowledge-point/canonical-example {:question/problem "Without using a calculator, find the exact value of $\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{24}\\right)}\\cos{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{24}\\right)}\\cos{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{12}\\right)}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\sin{x}\\cos{x} = \\sin{2x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{24}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n2\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{24}\\right)\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{24}\\right) = \\sin\\left(2\\cdot \\dfrac{\\pi}{24}\\right)\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n2\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{24}\\right)\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{24}\\right) = \\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{12}\\right) .\n$$\n\nNow, we can rewrite the given expression using the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{24})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{24})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12}) & =\\frac{1}{2}[2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{24})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{24})]\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the double-angle formula once more with $x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{12},$ we can further simplify the above expression: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12}) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}[2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{12})] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{24}\\right)}\\cos{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{24}\\right)}\\cos{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{12}\\right)} = \\dfrac{1}{8}.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Using the Double-Angle Formula Multiple Times"} {:knowledge-point/canonical-example {:question/problem "Simplify the expression $\\dfrac{\\sin 25^\\circ}{\\sin 50^\\circ}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n\\sin{2x} = 2\\sin{x}\\cos{x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=25^\\circ$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\sin\\left(2\\cdot 25^\\circ\\right) = 2\\sin 25^\\circ \\cos 25^\\circ\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\sin 50^\\circ = 2\\sin 25^\\circ \\cos 25^\\circ.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can simplify the given expression by rewriting the ${\\color{blue} \\sin 50^\\circ}$ in the denominator as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{25}^{∘}}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{50}^{∘}} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{25}^{∘}}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡{25}^{∘}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{25}^{∘}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{25}^{∘}}}{2\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{25}^{∘}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{25}^{∘}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡{25}^{∘}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡{25}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Using the Double-Angle Formula to Simplify Ratios"} {:knowledge-point/canonical-example {:question/problem "Without using a calculator, find the exact value of $3\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{8}\\right)}\\cos{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{8}\\right)}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\sin{x}\\cos{x} = \\sin 2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\dfrac {\\pi}{8}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n2\\sin \\left(\\dfrac {\\pi} {8} \\right) \\cos \\left(\\dfrac {\\pi} {8} \\right) = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the above by $\\dfrac 1 2$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8}) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8}) & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n3 \\, {\\color{black}\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{8}\\right)}\\cos{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{8}\\right)} } = 3 \\left( \\color{black} \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} \\right) = \\dfrac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding Exact Value of a Trigonometric Expression Using the Double-Angle Formula"}] :topic/math-academy-id 271 :topic/title "The Double-Angle Formula for Sine"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048703 :topic/knowledge-points [{:knowledge-point/canonical-example {:question/problem "Express $\\cos^2{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{24}\\right)} - \\sin^2{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{24}\\right)}$ as a single trigonometric ratio." :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos^2 x - \\sin^2 x = \\cos 2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={\\dfrac{\\pi}{24}}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{24})-{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{24}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{24}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Using the Double-Angle Formula to Simplify Expressions"} {:knowledge-point/canonical-example {:question/problem "Express $2\\cos^2{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right)}-1$ as a single trigonometric ratio." :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n2 \\cos^2 x - 1 = \\cos 2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})-1 & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Using Alternative Forms of Double-Angle Formula to Simplify Expressions"} {:knowledge-point/canonical-example {:question/problem "Without using a calculator, find the exact value of $\\cos^2\\left( 22.5^\\circ\\right) - \\sin^2\\left( 22.5^\\circ\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos^2 x - \\sin^2 x = \\cos 2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={22.5^\\circ}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡({22.5}^{∘})-{sin}^{2}⁡({22.5}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot {22.5}^{∘}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡{45}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding Exact Values of Trigonometric Expressions Using the Double-Angle Formula"}] :topic/math-academy-id 831 :topic/title "The Double-Angle Formula for Cosine"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048784 :topic/knowledge-points [{:knowledge-point/canonical-example {:question/problem "Solve $\\sqrt{2x-6} = 2$." :question/worked-solution "Squaring both sides of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{2x - 6} &= 2 \\\\\n(\\sqrt{2x - 6})^{2} &= 2^{2} \\\\\n2x - 6 &= 4 \\\\\n2x &= 10 \\\\\nx &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check the result by plugging it into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{2(5) - 6} &= ^(?)2 \\\\\n\\sqrt{4} &= ^(?)2 \\\\\n2 &= 2✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe value $x=5$ satisfies the original equation, so we can accept it as a solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution to\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{2x-6} = 2\n$$\n\nis $x=5$."} :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Radical Equation With an Isolated Radical"} {:knowledge-point/canonical-example {:question/problem "Which of the following equations have no real solution?\n\n1. $\\sqrt{5-x} = -2$\n2. $\\sqrt{4-x} = 5$\n3. $\\sqrt{2x+1} = -1$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square root is always positive or zero. So, a radical equation has no real solution if it states that a square root is equal to a negative number.\n\nOf the given equations, only I and III state that a square root is equal to a negative number. Therefore, equations I and III have no real solution.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Radical Equations With No Real Solution"} {:knowledge-point/canonical-example {:question/problem "Solve $\\sqrt{x^2-5x+8} -2 = 0$." :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the square root by adding $2$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{x^{2} - 5x + 8} - 2 &= 0 \\\\\n\\sqrt{x^{2} - 5x + 8} &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the square root is isolated, we can proceed as usual. Squaring both sides of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\sqrt{x^{2} - 5x + 8})^{2} &= (2)^{2} \\\\\nx^{2} - 5x + 8 &= 4 \\\\\nx^{2} - 5x + 4 &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} - 4x - x + 4 &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 4)(x - 1) &= 0 \\\\\n&= x = 4, 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check the results by plugging them into the original equation.\n\n- For $x=1$, we get\n$\\sqrt{1^{2} - 5(1) + 8} - 2|= ^(?)0; \\sqrt{4}|= ^(?)2; 2|= 2.✓$\n- For $x=4$, we get\n$\\sqrt{4^{2} - 5(4) + 8} - 2|= ^(?)0; \\sqrt{4}|= ^(?)2; 2|= 2.✓$\n\nBoth $x=1$ and $x=4$ satisfy the original equation, so we can accept them both as solutions.\n\nTherefore, the solutions to\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x^2-5x+8} -2 = 0\n$$\n\nare $x=1$ and $x=4$."} :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Radical Equation When the Radical Is Not Isolated"} {:knowledge-point/canonical-example {:question/problem "Solve $\\sqrt{x+10} = x-2$." :question/worked-solution "To solve the equation\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x+10} = x-2\n$$\n\nwe raise both sides to the power of $2$. This allows us to get rid of the radical, and gives us a quadratic equation that is easier to solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{x + 10} &= x - 2 \\\\\n(\\sqrt{x + 10})^{2} &= (x - 2)^{2} \\\\\nx + 10 &= x^{2} - 4x + 4 \\\\\nx^{2} - 5x - 6 &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} - 6x + x - 6 &= 0 \\\\\n(x + 1)(x - 6) &= 0 \\\\\nx &= -1, 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemember that in square root equations, we should always check the obtained value(s) to ensure that they are actually solutions of the original equation.\n\n- First, we check whether the obtained value $x=-1$ is a solution of the original equation:\n$\\sqrt{-1 + 10}|= ^(?) - 1 - 2; \\sqrt{9}|= ^(?) - 3; 3 \\mid \\ne - 3\\cdot$\nThe value $x=-1$ does *not* satisfy the original equation, so we discard it.\n- Second, we check whether the obtained value $x=6$ is a solution of the original equation:\n$\\sqrt{6 + 10}|= ^(?)6 - 2; \\sqrt{16}|= ^(?)4; 4|= 4✓$\nIndeed, the value $x=6$ does satisfy the original equation.\n\nTherefore, the only solution of\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x+10} = x-2\n$$\n\nis $x=6$."} :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Radical Equation With an Additional Linear Term"}] :topic/math-academy-id 116 :topic/title "Solving Radical Equations"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048826 :topic/knowledge-points [{:knowledge-point/canonical-example {:question/problem "Solve the following system of equations:\n${x - y = 4; x + 2y = 16$" :question/worked-solution "We will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. It doesn't matter which equation we pick first, so we choose the easiest one. In this case, the first equation looks like the easiest.\n\nUsing the first equation, we can isolate $x$ by adding $y$ to both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx - y &= 4 \\\\\nx &= 4 + y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we replace $x$ with $4+y$ in the second equation and solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} x+2y &=16 \\\\ (4+y) + 2y &=16 \\\\ 4+3y &=16 \\\\ 3y &=12 \\\\ y &=4 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $y=4$ into\n\n$$\nx=4+y\n$$\n\n, then we can find the value of $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} x &= 4 + y \\\\ &= 4 + (4) \\\\ &=8 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x = 8$ and $y = 4$, which can be written as $(8, 4)$."} :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Linear Equations Using Substitution"} {:knowledge-point/canonical-example {:question/problem "Solve the following system of equations:\n\n${2u + 9v - 6 = 0; 2u + 6v - 8 = 0$" :question/worked-solution "Sometimes, it's handy to assign a number to each equation in a system, so that we can refer to them more easily:\n\n$$\n{2u + 9v - 6 = 0(1); 2u + 6v - 8 = 0(2)\n$$\n\nWe will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. Using equation $\\color{blue}(2)$, we can solve for $u$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 2u+6v-8 &= 0 \\\\ 2u+6v &= 8 \\\\ 2u &= 8 - 6v \\\\ u &= \\dfrac{8-6v}{2} \\\\ u &= 4 - 3v \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $u=4-3v$ into equation $\\color{red}(1)$ and solve for $v$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 2u+9v-6 &= 0 \\\\ 2(4-3v)+9v-6 &= 0 \\\\ 8-6v+9v-6 &= 0 \\\\ 2+3v &= 0 \\\\ 3v &= -2 \\\\ v &= -\\dfrac{2}{3} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute\n\n$$\nv=-\\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\ninto equation $\\color{red}(1)$, then we can find the value of $u$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 2u+9v-6 &= 0 \\\\ 2u+9\\left(-\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right) -6 &= 0 \\\\ 2u-6-6 &= 0 \\\\ 2u-12 &= 0 \\\\ 2u &= 12 \\\\ u &= 6 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $u=6$ and\n\n$$\nv=-\\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\n, which can be written as\n\n$$\n\\left(6,-\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right)\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Challenging System of Linear Equations Using Substitution"}] :topic/math-academy-id 487 :topic/title "Solving Systems of Equations by Substitution"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048836 :topic/knowledge-points [{:knowledge-point/canonical-example {:question/problem "Express $\\tan^2 x\\sec^2 x - \\tan^4 x +1$ in terms of $\\sec x$ only." :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the different forms of the secant-tangent Identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & (1) & 1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & (2) & {tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x-1 \\\\  & (3) & {sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{2}⁡x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite our expression using $(3),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡x{sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{4}⁡x+1 & ={tan}^{2}⁡x({sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{2}⁡x)+1 \\\\  & ={tan}^{2}⁡x\\cdot (1)+1 \\\\  & ={tan}^{2}⁡x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can use $(1),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡x+1 & ={sec}^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\tan^2 x\\sec^2 x - \\tan^4 x +1 = \\sec^2 x.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying an Expression by Factoring and Using Various Forms of the Secant-Tangent Identity"} {:knowledge-point/canonical-example {:question/problem "Simplify $\\dfrac{1}{\\sec^2 \\theta - \\tan^2 \\theta}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the different forms of the secant-tangent Identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & (1) & 1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & (2) & {tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1 \\\\  & (3) & {sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite our expression using $(3),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}\\sec^2 \\theta - \\tan^2 \\theta} =  \\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue} 1} = 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\sec^2 \\theta - \\tan^2 \\theta} = 1.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying an Expression Using the Alternate Forms of the Secant-Tangent Identity"} {:knowledge-point/canonical-example {:question/problem "Simplify $\\dfrac{1}{\\sec\\theta + \\tan\\theta}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the different forms of the secant-tangent Identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & (1) & 1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & (2) & {tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1 \\\\  & (3) & {sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite our expression using $(3),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}1}{\\sec{\\theta}+\\tan{\\theta}} = \\dfrac{\\color{blue}\\sec^2{\\theta} - \\tan^2{\\theta}}{\\sec{\\theta} + \\tan{\\theta}}\n$$\n\nNext, we can further simplify our expression by factoring the numerator as a difference of squares, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta )(\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta )}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta )\\cancel{(\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta )}}{\\cancel{(\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta )}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\sec\\theta + \\tan\\theta} =  \\sec{\\theta} - \\tan{\\theta}.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying an Expression by Factoring Alternative Forms of the Secant-Tangent Identity"}] :topic/math-academy-id 3857 :topic/title "Alternate Forms of the Secant-Tangent Identity"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050107 :topic/knowledge-points [{:knowledge-point/canonical-example {:question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\sin \\theta \\, (1 + \\cot^2 \\theta)$." :question/worked-solution "First, using the identity $1 + \\cot^2 \\theta = \\csc^2 \\theta,$ we rewrite the expression in the parentheses: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,(1+{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot {csc}^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the fact that $\\csc {\\theta} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin{\\theta}},$ we can further simplify the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot {csc}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying an Expression Using the Cotangent-Cosecant Identity"} {:knowledge-point/canonical-example {:question/problem "Simplify $\\dfrac{1}{\\csc \\theta - \\cot \\theta}.$" :question/worked-solution "Note that we can simplify this expression by transforming the denominator into a form where we can apply a known trigonometric identity.\n\nFirst, we multiply the numerator and the denominator of our expression by $\\csc \\theta + \\cot \\theta.$ This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }{{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta -{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the identity $\\csc^2 \\theta - \\cot^2 \\theta=1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }{{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta -{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }{1} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying an Expression Using the Alternate Forms of the Cotangent-Cosecant Identity"} {:knowledge-point/canonical-example {:question/problem "Simplify $\\csc x  + \\csc x \\cot^2 x  -\\csc^3x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor $\\csc x$ from the first two terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+\\operatorname{csc}⁡x{cot}^{2}⁡x-{csc}^{3}⁡x & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\,(1+{cot}^{2}⁡x)-{csc}^{3}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the cotangent-cosecant identity,\n\n$$\n1+ \\cot^2 x=\\csc^2 x ,\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\,(1+{cot}^{2}⁡x)-{csc}^{3}⁡x & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\cdot ({csc}^{2}⁡x)-{csc}^{3}⁡x \\\\  & ={csc}^{3}⁡x-{csc}^{3}⁡x \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying an Expression by Factoring and Using Various Forms of the Cotangent-Cosecant Identity"} {:knowledge-point/canonical-example {:question/problem "Simplify $\\dfrac{1}{\\csc \\theta + \\cot \\theta} + \\cot \\theta.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following identity: \n\n$$\n1= \\csc^2{\\theta} -\\cot^2{\\theta}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above identity into the given expression, and then factoring it as a difference of squares, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }+\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta -{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }+\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta )\\,(\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta )}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }+\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta )\\,\\cancel{(\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta )}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }}+\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta )+\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying an Expression by Factoring the Alternate Forms of the Cotangent-Cosecant Identity"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1455 :topic/title "Simplifying Trigonometric Expressions Using the Cotangent-Cosecant Identity"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1255 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048858 :topic/math-academy-id 873 :topic/title "Parametric Equations of Horizontal Hyperbolas"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048918 :topic/math-academy-id 2981 :topic/title "Parametric Equations of Vertical Hyperbolas"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049212 :topic/math-academy-id 3145 :topic/title "Properties of Line Integrals of Scalar Functions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050722 :topic/math-academy-id 806 :topic/title "Parametric Equations of Circles"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050745 :topic/math-academy-id 6355 :topic/title "Clairaut Differential Equations"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050769 :topic/math-academy-id 875 :topic/title "Parametric Equations of Parabolas"}] :topic/title "Cartesian Equations of Parametric Curves"}]}
